@page "/"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@inject ICurrentUser Caller
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

<PageTitle>DriveTrack</PageTitle>

@* FR-99: one landing page, two audiences.

   The hero states what the product is and offers the way in; the three cards below say what each
   role actually does with it, which is the question a visitor arrives with. The design draws only
   the anonymous state, and the signed-in one survives beside it: anyone with a session is offered
   the way into the application instead of the two entry points, and where that is belongs to
   LandingRoute, which is the one place that knows where a role lands.

   NFR-24: every action carries an icon AND its text. An icon on its own is a guess.

   Independent @if blocks rather than if/else, and ICurrentUser rather than <AuthorizeView>, so the
   signed-in branch can read the role it needs without a second notion of who the caller is. *@

<div class="dt-landing">
    <section class="dt-landing-hero">
        <div class="dt-landing-pitch">
            <div class="dt-landing-eyebrow">
                <DtBadge Variant="DtBadgeVariant.Role">
                    <Icon Name="IconName.Vehicles" />@Localizer["HomeHeroBadge"]
                </DtBadge>
            </div>

            <h1 class="dt-type-display dt-landing-title">@Localizer["HomeHeroTitle"]</h1>

            <p class="dt-landing-lead dt-muted">@Localizer["HomeStatus"]</p>

            @if (Caller.IsAuthenticated)
            {
                <div class="dt-landing-actions">
                    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="@LandingRoute.For(Caller.Role)">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Home" />@Localizer["GoToApp"]
                    </a>
                </div>
            }

            @if (!Caller.IsAuthenticated)
            {
                <div class="dt-landing-actions">
                    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="register">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Register" />@Localizer["Register"]
                    </a>
                    <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="sign-in">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.SignIn" />@Localizer["SignIn"]
                    </a>
                </div>
            }

            <p class="dt-landing-note dt-type-small dt-muted">@Localizer["HomeHeroNote"]</p>
        </div>

        @* An illustration of the product rather than a piece of it: three invented deliveries, drawn
           in the real vocabulary - the status labels a dispatcher reads, the overdue badge, the
           column headings the dispatch board uses - so what a visitor is shown is what they will
           later see. Hidden from assistive technology for the same reason: a screen reader has
           nothing to do with a picture of a table whose rows do not exist, and the sentence beside
           it already says what the product does.

           The rows are written as literals rather than as resource keys. The addresses and the time
           windows are decorative filler, and a key apiece would put invented data in the
           translation catalogue, where a translator would have to render three Kyiv street names
           into English - which is not a thing that should happen to a street name. *@
        <div class="dt-landing-preview" aria-hidden="true">
            @* The card head carries its title and nothing else. The design draws a create action
               beside it, and this is the one part of the illustration that could not be borrowed:
               painted in the real create colour it is pixel-identical to the button that makes a
               delivery, and `aria-hidden` keeps it from assistive technology but not from a thumb -
               on a phone it lands directly under the page's own call to action, where a tap that
               does nothing is the likeliest tap on the screen. A board with its rows is still
               recognisably the board. *@
            <DtCard Title="@Localizer["DeliveriesTitle"]">
                <ChildContent>
                    <table class="dt-landing-board dt-type-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>@Localizer["DeliveryWindow"]</th>
                                <th>@Localizer["Dropoff"]</th>
                                <th>@Localizer["Status"]</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>
                                    09:00–11:00
                                    <DtBadge Variant="DtBadgeVariant.Overdue">@Localizer["Overdue"]</DtBadge>
                                </td>
                                <td>вул. Хрещатик, 22</td>
                                <td><DeliveryStatusLabel Status="DeliveryStatus.InTransit" /></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>11:00–13:00</td>
                                <td>просп. Перемоги, 5</td>
                                <td><DeliveryStatusLabel Status="DeliveryStatus.Pending" /></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>13:00–15:00</td>
                                <td>вул. Саксаганського, 40</td>
                                <td><DeliveryStatusLabel Status="DeliveryStatus.Delivered" /></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </ChildContent>
            </DtCard>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* One card per role that uses the product, in the order the work runs: dispatch plans it, a
       driver carries it, a client receives it. The glyph in each head is the one the navigation
       uses for that role's own screens, so the card and the destination it describes are recognisably
       the same thing. *@
    <section class="dt-landing-roles">
        <DtCard Title="@Localizer["HomeForDispatchers"]">
            <Actions>
                <Icon Name="IconName.Dispatchers" />
            </Actions>
            <ChildContent>
                <p class="dt-landing-role-body dt-muted">@Localizer["HomeForDispatchersBody"]</p>
            </ChildContent>
        </DtCard>

        <DtCard Title="@Localizer["HomeForDrivers"]">
            <Actions>
                <Icon Name="IconName.Drivers" />
            </Actions>
            <ChildContent>
                <p class="dt-landing-role-body dt-muted">@Localizer["HomeForDriversBody"]</p>
            </ChildContent>
        </DtCard>

        <DtCard Title="@Localizer["HomeForClients"]">
            <Actions>
                <Icon Name="IconName.Clients" />
            </Actions>
            <ChildContent>
                <p class="dt-landing-role-body dt-muted">@Localizer["HomeForClientsBody"]</p>
            </ChildContent>
        </DtCard>
    </section>
</div>
